  { id: "mhp-pratapgad", type: "event", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Pratapgad and the meeting with Afzal Khan", value: 5, cue: "Mahabaleshwar trips, Shivaji Maharaj, or a risky face-to-face meeting",
    emoji: "🏰", wiki: "Pratapgad",
    fact: "Pratapgad fort near Mahabaleshwar was built in 1656 on the orders of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj. On 10 November 1659, the Bijapur general Afzal Khan came to meet him at the foot of the fort. The meeting ended with Afzal Khan dead and his army routed.",
    why: "It was the first great victory of the young Maratha state. After it, Shivaji Maharaj was treated as a serious power, not a local rebel.",
    mech: "Afzal Khan led a large Bijapur army, far bigger than the Maratha force. Shivaji Maharaj chose to meet him on his own ground, in a tent below Pratapgad, with the hills and forest around. Both men came with a few guards. Accounts agree that the embrace turned into an attack, and that Shivaji Maharaj killed Khan with a wagh nakh, a steel tiger claw worn on the hand. He wore armour under his clothes. At a signal from the fort, Maratha troops hidden in the forest attacked the Bijapur army, which broke and fled.",
    models: ["game-theory", "margin-of-safety"],
    q: "Why did the meeting at Pratapgad end so well for the Marathas?",
    a: "Shivaji Maharaj chose the ground, a tent below Pratapgad surrounded by hills and forest. He came prepared, with armour under his clothes and a wagh nakh. When Afzal Khan attacked, he struck back. Then hidden troops fell on the Bijapur army and routed it.",
    chain: [["Afzal Khan had the bigger army", ["bigger", "larger", "bijapur", "afzal"]], ["The meeting was on Maratha ground, below Pratapgad", ["tent", "pratapgad", "own ground", "chose", "forest", "hill"]], ["Armour and the wagh nakh", ["armour", "armor", "wagh nakh", "tiger claw", "prepared"]], ["Hidden troops routed the army", ["hidden", "signal", "routed", "fled", "ambush"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Where did Shivaji Maharaj meet Afzal Khan in 1659?", o: ["In a tent at the foot of Pratapgad fort", "Inside the Bijapur palace", "On a ship off the Konkan coast", "At the Mughal court in Agra"] },
    bring: "When someone faces a difficult meeting: “Shivaji Maharaj met Afzal Khan on his own ground, with armour under his shirt. Choose the place, and prepare for the worst.”",
    follow: "In a negotiation, how much does the choice of place matter?",
    deeper: "The fort was built by Moropant Pingle to guard the Par pass and the Koyna valley. A statue of Shivaji Maharaj on horseback was unveiled at the fort in 1957. Afzal Khan's tomb still stands below the fort." },

  { id: "mhp-pavan-khind", type: "historical story", domain: "strategy", region: "maharashtra", title: "Baji Prabhu at Pavan Khind", value: 5, cue: "Kolhapur, holding on against odds, or sacrifice for a team", story: "mhp-pavankhind",
    emoji: "⚔️", wiki: "Pawankhind",
    fact: "In 1660 the Bijapur general Siddi Johar trapped Shivaji Maharaj inside Panhala fort near Kolhapur for months. On a monsoon night, Shivaji Maharaj slipped out and raced for Vishalgad. Baji Prabhu Deshpande and about 300 men held a narrow pass, Ghod Khind, until he reached safety. Baji Prabhu died there on 13 July 1660.",
    why: "A few hundred men bought the time that saved the Maratha state. The pass was renamed Pavan Khind, the holy pass.",
    mech: "The siege of Panhala lasted about four months. The escape was planned for a night of heavy rain, when the besiegers were careless. The party of about 600 was soon spotted and chased. Baji Prabhu split the force. Half held the narrow pass, where a few men could block a whole army. The rest went on with Shivaji Maharaj. The plan was that cannon fire from Vishalgad would signal that the king was safe. Baji Prabhu is said to have fought on with wounds until he heard the guns.",
    models: ["bottlenecks", "opportunity-cost"],
    q: "How did Baji Prabhu's stand at the pass save Shivaji Maharaj?",
    a: "After escaping the siege of Panhala on a rainy night, the party was chased. Baji Prabhu held the narrow pass with about 300 men, where a small force could stop an army, while Shivaji Maharaj rode on to Vishalgad. Cannon fire from the fort signalled he was safe. Baji Prabhu died on 13 July 1660.",
    chain: [["The escape from the siege of Panhala", ["panhala", "siege", "escape", "rain"]], ["A narrow pass where few can stop many", ["narrow", "pass", "khind", "block"]], ["About 300 men held it while the king rode on", ["300", "held", "vishalgad", "rode on"]], ["Cannon fire signalled safety; Baji Prabhu died", ["cannon", "gun", "signal", "died", "13 july"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why was Ghod Khind a good place to make a stand?", o: ["It was a narrow pass where a few men could block a whole army", "It had a large fort with cannons", "It was on the sea coast", "It was flat open ground good for cavalry"] },
    bring: "When a team member covers for others: “That is Baji Prabhu at Pavan Khind. Hold the gap so the rest can get through.”",
    follow: "Is there a narrow point in your work where one person holds everything up?",
    deeper: "The escape is also linked to Shiva Kashid, a barber who is said to have dressed as Shivaji Maharaj to mislead the pursuers. This part rests on tradition more than records. Panhala fort is one of the largest in the Deccan, and Kolhapur sits only about 20 km away." },

  { id: "mhp-sinhagad", type: "historical story", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Sinhagad: the fort won, the lion lost", value: 5, cue: "Pune weekend treks, Tanaji, or a win that came at a heavy price", story: "mhp-sinhagad",
    emoji: "🦁", wiki: "Sinhagad",
    fact: "On the night of 4 February 1670, Tanaji Malusare led a small Maratha force up the cliffs of Kondhana fort near Pune. The fort was held for the Mughals by Udaybhan Rathod. The Marathas won, but Tanaji was killed. The fort was then named Sinhagad, the lion's fort.",
    why: "Shivaji Maharaj had been forced to give the fort away in 1665. Taking it back announced that the Maratha fight against the Mughals was on again.",
    mech: "Under the Treaty of Purandar in 1665, Shivaji Maharaj had to hand 23 forts to the Mughals, including Kondhana. Kondhana sits above Pune and controlled the land around the city. A direct attack on its gates was hopeless. So the Marathas climbed a steep cliff at night with ropes, where no guard expected them. In the fighting on the walls both Tanaji and Udaybhan were killed. Tanaji's brother Suryaji rallied the men and took the fort.",
    models: ["inversion", "opportunity-cost"],
    q: "How was Kondhana fort taken in 1670, and what did it cost?",
    a: "Shivaji Maharaj had given the fort to the Mughals under the Treaty of Purandar in 1665. Tanaji's men climbed a steep cliff at night with ropes, where no attack was expected. They won the fort, but Tanaji died, and it was renamed Sinhagad.",
    chain: [["Lost to the Mughals under the Treaty of Purandar in 1665", ["purandar", "1665", "treaty", "handed", "gave"]], ["A night climb up the cliff with ropes", ["cliff", "climb", "rope", "night"]], ["Tanaji was killed in the fight", ["tanaji", "killed", "died"]], ["Renamed Sinhagad, the lion's fort", ["sinhagad", "lion", "renamed"]]],
    mcq: { q: "How did the Marathas enter Kondhana fort in 1670?", o: ["They climbed a steep cliff at night with ropes", "They bribed the gatekeeper to open the main gate", "They dug a tunnel under the walls", "They starved the fort for a year"] },
    bring: "On the Sinhagad road: “The fort got its name from a defeat inside a victory. They took the fort but lost Tanaji.”",
    follow: "What win in your life cost more than you expected?",
    deeper: "The famous line “Gad ala, pan sinha gela” (the fort is won, but the lion is gone) is attributed to Shivaji Maharaj by tradition, not by a record of the time. The story of a monitor lizard called Yashwanti carrying the rope up the cliff is a popular legend. Lokmanya Tilak often stayed at Sinhagad, and his bungalow is still there." },

  { id: "mhp-daulatabad", type: "event", domain: "strategy", region: "maharashtra", title: "Daulatabad: a capital moved and moved back", value: 4, cue: "Aurangabad trips, head-office relocations, or a plan on paper that ignores people",
    emoji: "🏯", wiki: "Daulatabad Fort",
    fact: "In 1327 Sultan Muhammad bin Tughlaq moved his capital from Delhi to Devagiri, a hill fort in Maharashtra, and renamed it Daulatabad. He ordered much of Delhi's population to walk over 1,100 km to the new city. Within about seven years the capital was moved back to Delhi.",
    why: "The idea was not foolish on a map. The failure came from treating people like pieces to be moved.",
    mech: "Daulatabad sat near the centre of the Sultan's lands, which now reached deep into the south. From there he hoped to control the Deccan better. But the forced march killed many people. Daulatabad was far from the northern frontier, where attacks came from Central Asia. The dry Deccan could not easily feed a huge new city. So the Sultan reversed the order around 1334, and the people who had survived walked back.",
    models: ["second-order-thinking", "map-vs-territory"],
    q: "Why did Tughlaq's move of the capital to Daulatabad fail?",
    a: "Daulatabad was central on the map, so it looked good for controlling the Deccan. But the forced march over 1,100 km killed many people, the city was far from the northern frontier, and the region could not feed it. Within about seven years the capital returned to Delhi.",
    chain: [["Daulatabad was central on the map", ["centre", "center", "central", "map", "deccan"]], ["People were forced to march over 1,100 km", ["forced", "march", "walk", "1,100"]], ["Far from the frontier and hard to feed", ["frontier", "north", "feed", "food", "dry"]], ["Reversed within about seven years", ["reverse", "moved back", "returned", "seven years", "1334"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why did Muhammad bin Tughlaq choose Daulatabad as capital?", o: ["It was near the centre of his lands, closer to the Deccan", "It had a sea port", "It was his birthplace", "Delhi had been destroyed by floods"] },
    bring: "When a company moves its head office: “Tughlaq moved his capital to Daulatabad because it was central on the map. He forgot the people who had to walk there.”",
    follow: "What decision looked perfect on paper but failed because of the people involved?",
    deeper: "Devagiri had been the capital of the Yadava kings before the Sultans took it in 1296. The fort's hill was cut into a smooth vertical wall, and the only way up is through a dark winding tunnel. The Chand Minar, a tall tower in the fort, was added by the Bahmani rulers in the 1400s." },

  { id: "mhp-bibi-ka-maqbara", type: "place", domain: "design", region: "maharashtra", title: "Bibi ka Maqbara: the Taj of the Deccan", value: 3, cue: "Aurangabad trips, the Taj Mahal, or copies that cost less",
    emoji: "🕌", wiki: "Bibi Ka Maqbara",
    fact: "Bibi ka Maqbara in Aurangabad (now Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar) is a tomb built in the 1660s for Dilras Banu Begum, the wife of Emperor Aurangzeb. It looks very much like the Taj Mahal. Records put its cost at about 6.7 lakh rupees, a small fraction of what the Taj cost.",
    why: "It shows what happens when a design is copied with far less money. The shape survives, the materials do not.",
    mech: "The Taj Mahal was built by Shah Jahan, Aurangzeb's father, in white marble from top to bottom. His grandson Azam Shah is usually credited with building this tomb for his mother. Aurangzeb was known for careful spending and long wars. So only the dome and the lower part of the tomb are marble. The rest is plaster over brick and stone, polished to look like marble. The tomb is also smaller than the Taj. From a distance the effect is close; up near, the difference shows.",
    models: ["opportunity-cost"],
    q: "Why does Bibi ka Maqbara look like the Taj Mahal but feel different up close?",
    a: "It was built in the 1660s for Aurangzeb's wife, copying the Taj Mahal's design. But the budget was only about 6.7 lakh rupees. So only the dome and lower parts are marble, and the rest is polished plaster over brick and stone.",
    chain: [["A tomb for Aurangzeb's wife, modelled on the Taj", ["aurangzeb", "dilras", "taj", "wife", "mother"]], ["Built in the 1660s with a small budget", ["1660", "6.7 lakh", "budget", "cost", "money"]], ["Only the dome and base are marble; the rest is plaster", ["marble", "plaster", "brick", "stucco"]], ["Close in look from far, different up close", ["distance", "close", "near", "smaller"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What is most of Bibi ka Maqbara made of?", o: ["Plaster over brick and stone, with marble only in parts", "Solid white marble throughout", "Red sandstone only", "Carved teak wood"] },
    bring: "When someone wants a cheaper copy of something famous: “Bibi ka Maqbara is the Taj at a fraction of the price. It looks right from far and plain from near.”",
    follow: "What copies have you seen that work from a distance but not up close?",
    deeper: "Aurangzeb's own grave is nearby at Khuldabad. By his wish it is a simple open grave with no dome, in sharp contrast to his wife's tomb." },

  { id: "mhp-shaniwar-wada", type: "fact", domain: "psychology", region: "maharashtra", title: "Shaniwar Wada: a fire, a murder and a ghost story", value: 4, cue: "Pune's old city, haunted places, or why some stories refuse to die",
    emoji: "🔥", wiki: "Shaniwar Wada",
    fact: "Shaniwar Wada in Pune was the palace of the Peshwas, finished in 1732 by Bajirao I. In 1773 the young Peshwa Narayanrao was murdered inside it by his own guards. On 27 February 1828 a fire broke out and burned for seven days. Only the stone walls and gates survived.",
    why: "People today call it Pune's most haunted place. The ghost is a legend, but the reasons the legend sticks are real and worth understanding.",
    mech: "Narayanrao was killed on the orders of his uncle Raghunathrao and aunt Anandibai. Tradition says he ran through the palace shouting “Kaka, mala vachva”, uncle, save me. A legend grew that his cries can still be heard on full moon nights. Two things keep such a story alive. First, a vivid, emotional event is remembered far more than ordinary facts. Second, an empty ruin at night gives the mind room to fill in what it expects to hear. There is no evidence of a ghost. There is strong evidence of a murder and a fire.",
    models: ["availability-bias", "confirmation-bias"],
    q: "What happened at Shaniwar Wada, and why does the ghost legend survive?",
    a: "The Peshwa palace, finished in 1732, saw the murder of Narayanrao by his guards in 1773 and a seven-day fire in 1828. The ghost story is a legend. It survives because a vivid emotional event is easy to recall, and an empty ruin at night lets people hear what they expect.",
    chain: [["The Peshwa palace, finished in 1732", ["1732", "peshwa", "bajirao", "palace"]], ["Narayanrao was murdered inside in 1773", ["1773", "narayanrao", "murder", "killed", "guards"]], ["A fire in 1828 burned for seven days", ["1828", "fire", "seven days", "burn"]], ["Vivid events and empty ruins keep the legend alive", ["vivid", "legend", "expect", "ghost", "recall", "remember"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Which of these about Shaniwar Wada is a documented fact, not a legend?", o: ["A fire in 1828 burned for seven days and gutted the palace", "Cries are heard on full moon nights", "The palace had seven underground floors", "Treasure is still buried under the gate"] },
    bring: "When someone says a place is haunted: “Shaniwar Wada had a real murder and a real fire. The ghost is just the way our memory keeps vivid stories alive.”",
    follow: "Why do you think scary stories spread faster than ordinary ones?",
    deeper: "The main gate is called Delhi Darwaza and faces north, a statement of Maratha ambition. Its doors carry long iron spikes to stop elephants from pushing them in. The palace once had a seven-storey building and gardens with a lotus-shaped fountain." },

  { id: "mhp-aga-khan-palace", type: "place", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Aga Khan Palace: a famine relief work turned prison", value: 4, cue: "Pune sightseeing, Quit India, or Kasturba Gandhi",
    emoji: "🏛️", wiki: "Aga Khan Palace",
    fact: "The Aga Khan Palace in Pune was built in 1892 by Sultan Muhammad Shah, Aga Khan III. He built it to give work to people hit by famine. In 1942 the British used it as a prison for Mahatma Gandhi, who was held there from 9 August 1942 to 6 May 1944. Kasturba Gandhi died there in February 1944.",
    why: "One building carries two stories: a rich man's answer to a famine, and the place where Gandhi lost his wife and his closest helper.",
    mech: "The Quit India movement was launched in Bombay on 8 August 1942. The next morning the British arrested Gandhi and other leaders. They wanted him away from crowds but not in a common jail, which would cause anger. So they chose a private palace in Pune. His secretary Mahadev Desai died there within days, on 15 August 1942. Kasturba, who had been ill for long, died on 22 February 1944. Their memorials stand in the palace garden. In 1969 Aga Khan IV gave the palace to India.",
    models: ["second-order-thinking"],
    q: "What is the Aga Khan Palace, and why is it tied to Gandhi?",
    a: "Aga Khan III built it in 1892 as famine relief work. After Quit India began in August 1942, the British held Gandhi there until May 1944. Mahadev Desai and Kasturba Gandhi both died there, and their memorials are in the garden. It was given to India in 1969.",
    chain: [["Built in 1892 as famine relief work", ["1892", "famine", "work", "aga khan"]], ["Gandhi was held there after Quit India in 1942", ["quit india", "1942", "arrest", "prison", "held"]], ["Mahadev Desai and Kasturba died there", ["kasturba", "mahadev", "died"]], ["Given to India in 1969", ["1969", "gave", "given", "donated"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why was the Aga Khan Palace originally built?", o: ["To give work to people suffering in a famine", "As a summer palace for the British Governor", "As a hotel for railway passengers", "As a hospital during the plague"] },
    bring: "When Pune sightseeing comes up: “The Aga Khan Palace was built to give famine work in 1892. Fifty years later it was Gandhi's prison.”",
    follow: "Why would the British choose a palace, not a jail, for Gandhi?",
    deeper: "Some of Gandhi's ashes are kept at the palace. The building houses a museum of his life, and the Gandhi National Memorial Society runs it. The design mixes Italian arches with wide Indian verandahs." },

  { id: "mhp-karla-bhaja", type: "place", domain: "economics", region: "maharashtra", title: "Karla and Bhaja: monks on the trade road", value: 4, cue: "Lonavala trips, Buddhist caves, or why temples stand where they do",
    emoji: "🪨", wiki: "Karla Caves",
    fact: "Near Lonavala, two groups of Buddhist caves are cut into the hills. Bhaja dates from about the 2nd century BCE. Karla's great prayer hall, about 45 metres long, is the largest rock-cut hall of its kind in India. It was finished around 2,000 years ago.",
    why: "The monks chose the spot for a reason that has nothing to do with quiet. They sat beside one of the busiest trade roads of their time.",
    mech: "Goods from ports on the Konkan coast had to climb the Western Ghats to reach the Deccan plateau. The Bhor Ghat near Lonavala was one of the few easy routes up. Traders passed in large numbers. Monasteries beside the road gave shelter to travellers and received gifts in return. Inscriptions at Karla record donations from merchants, bankers and even foreign traders called Yavanas. Trade paid for the caves, and the caves served the trade.",
    models: ["network-effects", "incentives"],
    q: "Why were the Karla and Bhaja caves built where they are?",
    a: "They sit beside the Bhor Ghat, one of the few easy climbs from the Konkan ports to the Deccan. Traders passed in large numbers. Monasteries beside the road sheltered travellers and received donations, which inscriptions at Karla record from merchants and bankers.",
    chain: [["Buddhist caves near Lonavala, about 2,000 years old", ["lonavala", "2,000", "bhaja", "karla", "2nd century"]], ["Beside the Bhor Ghat trade route from the coast", ["bhor ghat", "trade", "route", "road", "ghats", "climb"]], ["Monasteries sheltered travellers", ["shelter", "traveller", "rest"]], ["Merchants paid for the caves", ["donat", "merchant", "banker", "gift", "inscription"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Who paid for much of the carving at Karla, according to its inscriptions?", o: ["Merchants, bankers and traders using the route", "The British East India Company", "Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj", "The Mughal emperors"] },
    bring: "When passing Lonavala on the expressway: “The Karla caves are here because this was the trade road up the Ghats 2,000 years ago. Same route, same reason.”",
    follow: "What modern businesses grow up beside a busy road for the same reason?",
    deeper: "The Bhaja prayer hall copies wooden buildings in stone, down to the ribs of the roof. Some of Bhaja's wooden beams, over 2,000 years old, are still in place. The railway and the expressway climb the same Bhor Ghat today." },

  { id: "mhp-shekru", type: "fact", domain: "science", region: "maharashtra", title: "The shekru: Maharashtra's giant squirrel", value: 3, cue: "Bhimashankar treks, state symbols, or forest animals",
    emoji: "🐿️", wiki: "Indian giant squirrel",
    fact: "The Indian giant squirrel, called shekru in Marathi, is the state animal of Maharashtra. Its body and tail can stretch close to a metre. Its fur is a mix of deep red, brown and cream. The Bhimashankar Wildlife Sanctuary near Pune was set up mainly to protect it.",
    why: "A squirrel as long as your arm lives a few hours from Mumbai, and most people have never seen one.",
    mech: "The shekru lives high in the trees and almost never comes to the ground. It leaps from branch to branch, sometimes six metres at a time. Each squirrel builds several large nests of leaves and twigs, so a predator cannot be sure which one is in use. A forest department survey at Bhimashankar counted over 10,000 nests but only about 1,300 to 1,700 squirrels. Because it needs tall, joined-up forest, cutting even a strip of trees can split its home in two.",
    models: ["margin-of-safety"],
    q: "What is the shekru, and why does it need unbroken forest?",
    a: "It is the Indian giant squirrel, Maharashtra's state animal, close to a metre long and found at Bhimashankar. It lives high in the trees, leaps between branches and keeps several nests as a safety measure. Because it rarely touches the ground, a gap in the forest cuts its home in two.",
    chain: [["State animal, close to a metre long", ["state animal", "metre", "meter", "giant"]], ["Lives high in the trees, rarely on the ground", ["tree", "canopy", "ground", "leap"]], ["Keeps several nests for safety", ["nest", "several", "many"]], ["Needs unbroken forest", ["unbroken", "joined", "gap", "cut", "split", "strip"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why does one shekru build several nests?", o: ["So a predator cannot be sure which nest it is using", "To store food for the winter", "Because the nests fall down every week", "To house its large family groups"] },
    bring: "On a Bhimashankar trek: “Look up. The state animal lives here, a squirrel almost a metre long, and it keeps spare nests to fool predators.”",
    follow: "Which Indian state animals can you name?",
    deeper: "Bhimashankar is also one of the twelve jyotirlingas and the source of the Bhima river. The giant squirrel is found only in India, mostly in the Western Ghats and the forests of central and eastern India." },

  { id: "mhp-mahabaleshwar", type: "place", domain: "geography", region: "maharashtra", title: "Mahabaleshwar: five rivers and most of India's strawberries", value: 4, cue: "weekend trips, strawberries, or where the Krishna river begins",
    emoji: "🍓", wiki: "Mahabaleshwar",
    fact: "Mahabaleshwar sits at about 1,350 metres in the Sahyadris. The Panchganga temple in Old Mahabaleshwar marks the traditional source of five rivers, including the Krishna and the Koyna. The area grows about 85% of India's strawberries, and the fruit got a GI tag in 2010.",
    why: "A small hill town feeds a river that reaches the Bay of Bengal, and a fruit that reaches every Mumbai supermarket.",
    mech: "The hill top catches very heavy monsoon rain, around 6,000 mm a year. Water soaks into the rock and comes out as springs, which become the Krishna, Koyna, Venna, Savitri and Gayatri. The Krishna then flows about 1,400 km east across the Deccan. For strawberries, the key is the cool, dry winter after the rains. The British planted the first strawberries here. Farmers now plant fresh runners each year and sell most of the crop between December and March.",
    models: ["bottlenecks"],
    q: "Why does Mahabaleshwar matter for both rivers and strawberries?",
    a: "Its hill top at about 1,350 metres gets very heavy monsoon rain, which feeds springs that start the Krishna and four other rivers. The cool, dry winter that follows is right for strawberries, so the area grows about 85% of India's crop.",
    chain: [["A hill top at about 1,350 metres with heavy rain", ["1,350", "hill", "rain", "6,000", "monsoon"]], ["Springs start the Krishna and four other rivers", ["krishna", "five rivers", "spring", "source", "panchganga"]], ["A cool dry winter suits strawberries", ["cool", "winter", "dry", "climate"]], ["About 85% of India's strawberries", ["85", "strawberr", "most of india"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Which major river has its traditional source at Mahabaleshwar?", o: ["The Krishna", "The Godavari", "The Narmada", "The Tapi"] },
    bring: "When eating strawberries: “Most likely from Mahabaleshwar. About 85% of India's strawberries come from there.”",
    follow: "Have you been to the Panchganga temple where the Krishna begins?",
    deeper: "Wilson Point, the highest spot, is about 1,440 metres. The Savitri flows west to the Arabian Sea while the Krishna flows east, so one hill sends water to both coasts. Strawberry plants here are not grown from seed but from runners, often first raised in cooler countries." },

  { id: "mhp-matheran", type: "place", domain: "design", region: "maharashtra", title: "Matheran: the hill station with no cars", value: 4, cue: "quick getaways from Mumbai, toy trains, or car-free streets",
    emoji: "🚂", wiki: "Matheran",
    fact: "Matheran, about 90 km from Mumbai, is often called Asia's only automobile-free hill station. Motor vehicles are banned inside the town. People walk, ride horses or take hand-pulled rickshaws. A narrow-gauge toy train, opened in 1907, climbs about 20 km from Neral to the top.",
    why: "It shows what a town feels like when the car is removed: red earth roads, birdsong, and no horns.",
    mech: "The hill was found by a British officer, Hugh Malet, in 1850, and became a summer retreat. The railway was built by Adamjee Peerbhoy, a Bombay businessman, and finished in 1907. The town's narrow roads and steep sides never suited cars. In 2003 the central government declared Matheran an eco-sensitive zone and barred motor vehicles. Visitors leave their cars at Dasturi car park, about 2.5 km below the market. The ban keeps the air clean and the forest quiet, but it also limits how much the town can grow.",
    models: ["second-order-thinking"],
    q: "How does Matheran stay free of cars, and what does this do to the town?",
    a: "A toy train built in 1907 by Adamjee Peerbhoy climbs from Neral to the hill. In 2003 the government made Matheran an eco-sensitive zone and banned motor vehicles. Visitors park at Dasturi and go on by foot, horse or rickshaw. The town stays quiet and clean, but cannot grow much.",
    chain: [["Toy train from Neral, built in 1907", ["1907", "neral", "toy train", "peerbhoy", "narrow"]], ["Eco-sensitive zone, cars banned", ["2003", "eco", "ban", "no cars", "motor"]], ["People walk, ride horses or take rickshaws", ["walk", "horse", "rickshaw", "dasturi", "foot"]], ["Quiet and clean, but limited growth", ["quiet", "clean", "limit", "grow"]]],
    mcq: { q: "How do visitors move around inside Matheran?", o: ["On foot, by horse or in hand-pulled rickshaws", "By electric buses", "By auto-rickshaws only", "By cable car"] },
    bring: "When someone wants a quick break from Mumbai: “Matheran. No cars at all, a 1907 toy train, and red mud roads.”",
    follow: "Could any part of Mumbai work without cars?",
    deeper: "Matheran means something like “forest on the forehead” in Marathi. The toy train has about 120 bends and climbs around 700 metres. The hill is a flat-topped plateau of the Deccan Traps, and there are over thirty viewpoints along its edge." },

  { id: "mhp-chikki", type: "business concept", domain: "business", region: "maharashtra", title: "Lonavala chikki: a railway snack", value: 3, cue: "Lonavala, train journeys, or how a local product becomes a brand",
    emoji: "🥜", wiki: "Lonavala chikki",
    fact: "Chikki, the sweet of jaggery and groundnuts, became famous in Lonavala. The Maganlal shop near the station dates from the 1880s. The sweet was sold to the workers building the railway through the Ghats, and then to the passengers who rode it.",
    why: "A simple sweet became a brand because it stood at the one place where everyone had to stop.",
    mech: "In the 1800s Lonavala was a work camp for the railway climbing the Bhor Ghat. Workers needed cheap, filling food that did not spoil in the heat. Jaggery and roasted groundnuts do both. Later every train between Bombay and Pune stopped at Lonavala. Passengers bought a packet to carry home. Over time the name of the town and the name of the sweet joined together. Today dozens of shops sell chikki along the old Mumbai-Pune road.",
    models: ["bottlenecks", "network-effects"],
    q: "Why is Lonavala famous for chikki?",
    a: "The sweet of jaggery and groundnuts was cheap, filling and did not spoil, so it suited the workers building the railway through the Ghats. Trains between Bombay and Pune then stopped at Lonavala, and passengers carried the packets home. The town's name became the brand.",
    chain: [["Jaggery and groundnut, cheap and long-lasting", ["jaggery", "groundnut", "peanut", "spoil", "cheap"]], ["Sold to railway workers in the Ghats", ["worker", "railway", "bhor ghat", "building"]], ["Trains stopped at Lonavala, passengers carried it home", ["stop", "passenger", "station", "carry"]], ["The town's name became the brand", ["brand", "name", "famous"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Who were the first big customers for Lonavala's chikki?", o: ["Workers building the railway through the Ghats", "British officers in Pune", "Pilgrims going to Pandharpur", "Film crews from Bombay"] },
    bring: "When someone brings chikki from Lonavala: “This started as food for the men who built the railway up the Ghats.”",
    follow: "Which other Indian towns are known for one food, and why?",
    deeper: "The word chikki is said to come from the Marathi word chikat, meaning sticky. The Bhor Ghat railway line opened in 1863 after years of hard work and many deaths among workers." },

  { id: "mhp-tadoba", type: "place", domain: "science", region: "maharashtra", title: "Tadoba: Maharashtra's tiger country", value: 4, cue: "wildlife trips, tiger sightings, or Chandrapur",
    emoji: "🐅", wiki: "Tadoba Andhari Tiger Reserve",
    fact: "Tadoba Andhari Tiger Reserve in Chandrapur district is Maharashtra's oldest national park, created in 1955. It is one of the best places in India to see a wild tiger. Scientific estimates have put its tiger numbers above 100, including the buffer forests around the core.",
    why: "Tigers here are doing so well that the problem has changed from saving them to finding them room.",
    mech: "Tadoba has dry teak and bamboo forest with lakes that hold water through summer. Deer and wild boar gather at the water, and tigers follow. Strict protection since Project Tiger, and the moving of some villages out of the core, let numbers grow. Young tigers must find their own territory, so many move into the buffer zones and nearby farms. This brings more sightings for tourists, and more conflict with people and cattle.",
    models: ["feedback-loops", "second-order-thinking"],
    q: "Why does Tadoba have so many tigers, and what new problem does that create?",
    a: "It is Maharashtra's oldest park, with teak and bamboo forest and lakes that keep prey nearby in summer. Protection and the moving of villages let tiger numbers grow above 100. Young tigers now spill into buffer zones and farms, so conflict with people rises.",
    chain: [["Oldest national park in Maharashtra, from 1955", ["1955", "oldest", "chandrapur"]], ["Forest and lakes that hold prey and water", ["lake", "water", "prey", "deer", "teak", "bamboo"]], ["Protection let numbers grow above 100", ["protect", "100", "grow", "village"]], ["Young tigers spill into buffer and farms, causing conflict", ["buffer", "farm", "conflict", "spill", "territory"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why do tiger sightings at Tadoba stay high even in summer?", o: ["Its lakes keep water and prey in one place", "The tigers are fed by forest guards", "The tigers are kept in large enclosures", "There are no trees to hide in"] },
    bring: "When a tiger safari comes up: “Tadoba is Maharashtra's best bet. Over a hundred tigers, and lakes that pull the animals together in summer.”",
    follow: "Where should young tigers go when a reserve is full?",
    deeper: "Chandrapur district has one of the highest numbers of tigers of any district in India, and also many coal mines and power plants. Maharashtra's other tiger reserves include Melghat, Pench, Nagzira-Navegaon, Bor and Sahyadri." },

  { id: "mhp-chikhaldara", type: "place", domain: "geography", region: "maharashtra", title: "Chikhaldara: Vidarbha's coffee hill", value: 3, cue: "Amravati, hill stations, or coffee grown in India",
    emoji: "☕", wiki: "Chikhaldara",
    fact: "Chikhaldara in Amravati district is the only hill station in Vidarbha, at about 1,100 metres in the Satpura range. It is the only place in Maharashtra where coffee is grown. The British began planting coffee here in the 1800s. The Melghat Tiger Reserve lies around it.",
    why: "People think of coffee as a southern crop. One cool hill in the hot heart of Vidarbha grows it too.",
    mech: "Coffee needs shade, cool air and good rain, which the hot plains of Vidarbha do not have. Chikhaldara's height gives cooler temperatures and over 1,500 mm of rain a year. The plateau also has steep slopes and tall trees for shade. The crop stayed small because the area is small and far from markets. Melghat, meaning the meeting of the ghats, was one of the first nine tiger reserves of 1973.",
    models: ["opportunity-cost"],
    q: "Why can Chikhaldara grow coffee when the rest of Vidarbha cannot?",
    a: "It sits at about 1,100 metres in the Satpura hills, so it is cooler and gets over 1,500 mm of rain, with tall trees for shade. The British planted coffee there in the 1800s. It stays a small crop because the area is small, and the Melghat Tiger Reserve surrounds it.",
    chain: [["A hill at about 1,100 metres in the Satpuras", ["1,100", "satpura", "hill", "height", "amravati"]], ["Cool air, good rain and shade", ["cool", "rain", "shade", "1,500"]], ["Only coffee-growing place in Maharashtra, since British times", ["coffee", "british", "1800", "only"]], ["Surrounded by the Melghat Tiger Reserve", ["melghat", "tiger", "1973"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What crop makes Chikhaldara unusual in Maharashtra?", o: ["Coffee", "Tea", "Apples", "Saffron"] },
    bring: "When Indian coffee comes up: “Not only Coorg. Chikhaldara in Vidarbha grows coffee too, the only place in Maharashtra.”",
    follow: "What other crop would surprise you to find in Maharashtra?",
    deeper: "Melghat is home to the Korku tribal community. The forest owlet, a bird thought extinct for over a century, was found again in 1997 in the Satpura region and now lives in Melghat too. Chikhaldara's name is linked to Kichaka from the Mahabharata by local tradition." },

  { id: "mhp-nashik-wine", type: "business concept", domain: "business", region: "maharashtra", title: "Nashik: India's wine capital", value: 4, cue: "wine, Nashik, or a new industry built from scratch",
    emoji: "🍇", wiki: "Sula Vineyards",
    fact: "Nashik district makes most of India's wine, often estimated at around 80%. Sula Vineyards, started in 1999 by Rajeev Samant, is the country's largest wine maker. Before the 1990s almost no wine was made in the area.",
    why: "A whole new industry grew in one generation, in a place already known for grapes but not for wine.",
    mech: "Nashik's farmers had grown table grapes for decades. The area sits at about 600 metres, with warm days and cool nights in winter, which helps grapes keep their flavour. Samant returned from a job in California, saw the climate and planted wine grapes on family land. The state later helped with lower taxes and rules for wineries. Wine tourism added a second income from visitors. One success drew more wineries, and the district now has dozens.",
    models: ["first-principles", "network-effects"],
    q: "How did Nashik become India's wine capital?",
    a: "Farmers already grew table grapes, and the height of about 600 metres gives cool winter nights that suit wine grapes. Rajeev Samant started Sula in 1999 after seeing California. State support and wine tourism followed, and dozens of wineries clustered there. Nashik now makes most of India's wine.",
    chain: [["Already a grape-growing area", ["table grape", "grape", "farmer", "decades"]], ["Height gives cool nights for flavour", ["600", "cool", "night", "climate", "height"]], ["Sula started in 1999 after seeing California", ["sula", "1999", "samant", "california"]], ["State support and tourism drew a cluster of wineries", ["state", "tax", "tourism", "cluster", "dozens", "80"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What advantage did Nashik already have before wine making began?", o: ["Farmers had long grown table grapes there", "It had old French wine cellars", "It had a large port for export", "It had the cheapest land in India"] },
    bring: "When wine comes up: “Most Indian wine is from Nashik. The whole industry is younger than many of us.”",
    follow: "What other local crop could become a bigger industry with the right push?",
    deeper: "Sula listed on the stock exchanges in 2022. Harvest in Nashik happens from January to March, the opposite of Europe. Nashik is also one of the four Kumbh Mela cities and a big centre for onions and table grapes." },

  { id: "mhp-godavari", type: "fact", domain: "geography", region: "maharashtra", title: "Trimbakeshwar and the Godavari's source", value: 4, cue: "Nashik, river sources, or the Kumbh Mela",
    emoji: "🏞️", wiki: "Godavari River",
    fact: "The Godavari, India's second longest river at about 1,465 km, begins at Brahmagiri hill near Trimbakeshwar, about 30 km from Nashik. Trimbakeshwar is one of the twelve jyotirlingas. The river reaches the Bay of Bengal in Andhra Pradesh and is called the Dakshin Ganga, the Ganga of the south.",
    why: "A thin trickle on a hill in Maharashtra becomes a river that waters five states.",
    mech: "Brahmagiri stands near the crest of the Western Ghats, where the monsoon dumps heavy rain. The crest slopes gently east, so most water flows away from the sea and across the whole Deccan. Along the way the Pranhita, Indravati and other rivers join. Nearly half of the Godavari's basin lies in Maharashtra. The Kumbh Mela is held at Nashik and Trimbakeshwar about every twelve years, drawing crores of pilgrims to the river's upper course.",
    models: ["compounding"],
    q: "Where does the Godavari begin, and why does it flow east?",
    a: "It starts at Brahmagiri hill near Trimbakeshwar, about 30 km from Nashik. The Ghats' crest slopes gently east, so the water crosses the Deccan for about 1,465 km to the Bay of Bengal. Many rivers join it on the way, and Nashik hosts the Kumbh Mela on its banks.",
    chain: [["Source at Brahmagiri near Trimbakeshwar", ["brahmagiri", "trimbak", "nashik", "source"]], ["The Ghats slope east, so the river crosses the Deccan", ["east", "slope", "deccan", "bay of bengal"]], ["About 1,465 km, second longest in India", ["1,465", "second longest", "longest"]], ["Kumbh Mela at Nashik and Trimbakeshwar", ["kumbh", "pilgrim", "twelve years"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Where does the Godavari river begin?", o: ["At Brahmagiri hill near Trimbakeshwar, Nashik", "At Mahabaleshwar", "In the Satpura hills near Amravati", "In the Himalayas"] },
    bring: "When Nashik comes up: “The Godavari starts there as a trickle on Brahmagiri. By Andhra it is one of India's biggest rivers.”",
    follow: "Why do you think almost all the big rivers of the Deccan flow east?",
    deeper: "The Godavari basin drains about a tenth of India's land. The Jayakwadi dam near Paithan holds back its water for Marathwada. The Trimbakeshwar temple was rebuilt in the 1700s by Peshwa Balaji Bajirao." },

  { id: "mhp-shirdi", type: "place", domain: "anthropology", region: "maharashtra", title: "Shirdi: a small village and a vast trust", value: 4, cue: "pilgrimages, Sai Baba, or how temple towns work",
    emoji: "🙏", wiki: "Shirdi",
    fact: "Shirdi in Ahilyanagar district was a small village where the saint Sai Baba lived for about sixty years until his death on 15 October 1918. Today the Shri Saibaba Sansthan Trust that runs the shrine is among the richest religious trusts in India. In 2022 it received over 400 crore rupees in donations.",
    why: "Sai Baba owned almost nothing and asked for little. The town built around his memory now runs on a scale of a large company.",
    mech: "Sai Baba lived in a mosque, begged for food and taught that God is one. He drew both Hindu and Muslim followers, and his sayings were simple, such as “shraddha and saburi”, faith and patience. After his death, followers built the Samadhi Mandir and set up a trust in 1922. Devotion spread through books, films and temples in other cities. Lakhs of pilgrims now arrive every week. The trust uses donations to run hospitals, free meals for thousands a day, schools and lodging.",
    models: ["compounding", "network-effects"],
    q: "How did Shirdi grow from a village into one of India's biggest pilgrimage centres?",
    a: "Sai Baba lived simply in Shirdi for about sixty years, drawing Hindu and Muslim followers with a message of faith and patience. After his death in 1918, a trust was set up in 1922, and devotion spread through books, films and temples. The trust now receives over 400 crore rupees a year and runs hospitals and free meals.",
    chain: [["Sai Baba lived simply in Shirdi till 1918", ["1918", "simpl", "mosque", "sixty"]], ["Followers from both Hindu and Muslim communities", ["hindu", "muslim", "both", "one"]], ["A trust from 1922 and devotion spread by books, films and temples", ["trust", "1922", "book", "film", "spread", "temple"]], ["Over 400 crore a year, spent on hospitals and meals", ["400 crore", "hospital", "meal", "donation"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What does the Shirdi trust do with much of its income?", o: ["Runs hospitals, free meals, schools and lodging for pilgrims", "Invests it in the share market only", "Pays it to the state government", "Builds hotels in Mumbai"] },
    bring: "When someone plans a Shirdi trip: “The trust there received over 400 crore in 2022. It runs hospitals and feeds thousands every day.”",
    follow: "What makes some saints' places grow so much larger than others?",
    deeper: "Shirdi got its own airport in 2017. Little is known for certain about Sai Baba's birth or early life, and devotees of different faiths tell his story differently. The trust was formed under a court scheme and is overseen by the state." },

  { id: "mhp-kolhapur-kusti", type: "concept", domain: "anthropology", region: "maharashtra", title: "Kolhapur's wrestling pits", value: 4, cue: "Kolhapur, wrestling, or how a king builds a sporting culture",
    emoji: "🤼", wiki: "Khasbag Wrestling Stadium",
    fact: "Kolhapur is the heart of kusti, traditional Indian wrestling, in Maharashtra. Shahu Maharaj, who ruled Kolhapur from 1894 to 1922, built the Khasbag maidan, a red-earth wrestling arena finished in 1912 that holds around 30,000 people. Wrestlers still train in mud pits called talims across the city.",
    why: "A sport survives for a century not by chance. A king gave it a stadium, a diet and a reason to compete.",
    mech: "Shahu Maharaj was a wrestling fan and a social reformer. He invited famous wrestlers to Kolhapur, gave them land and food, and held bouts for the public. Wrestlers train in talims, where young men live together, eat a heavy diet of milk, almonds and ghee, and practise in soft red earth. The red earth cushions falls, so matches can be hard without bones breaking. Kolhapur produced India's first individual Olympic medal: Khashaba Jadhav, who trained here, won a bronze in wrestling in 1952.",
    models: ["incentives", "compounding"],
    q: "Why did Kolhapur become the home of wrestling in Maharashtra?",
    a: "Shahu Maharaj, who ruled from 1894 to 1922, supported wrestlers with land and food and built the 30,000-seat Khasbag arena in 1912. Wrestlers live and train in mud-pit talims on a heavy diet. The tradition produced Khashaba Jadhav, India's first individual Olympic medal in 1952.",
    chain: [["Shahu Maharaj backed wrestlers with land, food and bouts", ["shahu", "king", "land", "food", "patron", "1894"]], ["Khasbag arena built in 1912 for about 30,000", ["khasbag", "1912", "30,000", "arena", "stadium"]], ["Talims with mud pits and a heavy diet", ["talim", "mud", "red earth", "diet", "milk"]], ["Khashaba Jadhav's Olympic bronze in 1952", ["jadhav", "olympic", "1952", "bronze"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Who built Kolhapur's Khasbag wrestling arena?", o: ["Shahu Maharaj of Kolhapur", "The British Governor of Bombay", "Peshwa Bajirao I", "The Nizam of Hyderabad"] },
    bring: "When wrestling or the Olympics comes up: “India's first individual medal came from a Kolhapur wrestler in 1952. Shahu Maharaj built that culture a century ago.”",
    follow: "What does a city need so that a sport lasts a hundred years?",
    deeper: "Shahu Maharaj also reserved half of government jobs in his state for backward classes in 1902, long before independent India did. Kolhapur's Mahalaxmi temple, over a thousand years old, is one of the Shakti Peethas. The city's name is also on its chappals and its spicy misal." },

  { id: "mhp-janjira", type: "place", domain: "strategy", region: "maharashtra", title: "Murud-Janjira: the fort the Marathas never took", value: 5, cue: "Alibag-Murud trips, sea forts, or a rival you cannot beat",
    emoji: "🌊", wiki: "Murud-Janjira",
    fact: "Janjira is an island fort off Murud, south of Alibag. It was held by the Siddis, a community of African origin, for over 300 years. Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj attacked it many times and failed. His son Sambhaji Maharaj tried too. No one took it by force until it joined India in 1948.",
    why: "The fort that beat the Marathas sat only about 150 km from Mumbai. It shows how much one good position can be worth.",
    mech: "The fort fills a rock island about a kilometre from shore. Its walls rise straight from the sea, so there is no beach to land on and no ground to dig under. Strong currents and rocks kept large ships away. Inside, two freshwater ponds gave sweet water even though the sea was all around. The Siddis were good sailors and were backed in turn by Bijapur and the Mughals, so the fort was resupplied by sea. The Marathas built their own forts nearby, Padmadurg and later Kolaba at Alibag, to contain it rather than capture it.",
    models: ["moat", "margin-of-safety"],
    q: "Why could the Marathas never capture Janjira?",
    a: "The fort covers a rock island with walls rising from the sea, so there was nowhere to land or dig. Freshwater ponds inside let it survive sieges, and the Siddis' ships, backed by Bijapur and the Mughals, kept it supplied. Shivaji and Sambhaji Maharaj both failed, and the Marathas built Padmadurg to contain it instead.",
    chain: [["Walls rise straight from the sea on a rock island", ["island", "rock", "walls", "sea", "land", "no beach"]], ["Freshwater ponds inside", ["fresh", "sweet water", "pond", "well"]], ["Siddi ships and allies kept it supplied", ["ship", "sailor", "supplied", "bijapur", "mughal", "siddi"]], ["Marathas built Padmadurg to contain it", ["padmadurg", "contain", "kolaba", "nearby"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What let Janjira survive long sieges even though the sea surrounded it?", o: ["Freshwater ponds inside the fort walls", "A secret tunnel to the mainland", "Rain tanks on the roof only", "Food grown on the island's farms"] },
    bring: "On a trip to Murud: “That fort beat Shivaji Maharaj's navy. Sea walls, sweet water inside, and ships to resupply it.”",
    follow: "When you cannot beat a rival, how do you contain them instead?",
    deeper: "The fort is said to have had 22 bastions and still has large old cannons, including one called Kalal Bangdi. The Siddi state of Janjira became a princely state under the British and merged into India in 1948. Kolaba fort off Alibag, built in 1680-81, later became the base of the Maratha admiral Kanhoji Angre." },

  { id: "mhp-amboli", type: "place", domain: "science", region: "maharashtra", title: "Amboli: the wettest place in Maharashtra", value: 3, cue: "monsoon getaways, frogs, or Sindhudurg",
    emoji: "🐸", wiki: "Amboli, Maharashtra",
    fact: "Amboli, a small hill station in Sindhudurg district, gets about 7,500 mm of rain a year, the most anywhere in Maharashtra. Mumbai gets about 2,200 mm. Scientists keep finding new species of frogs and other small animals there.",
    why: "A place most people drive through on the way to Goa is one of the richest spots for life in the Western Ghats.",
    mech: "Amboli sits at about 690 metres on the edge of the Ghats, right where the monsoon clouds from the Arabian Sea first hit the hills. The air is forced up, cools, and drops its water. The result is months of near-constant rain and mist. Frogs, snakes and insects that need wet skin and wet ground thrive. Many live only in a small area, so a new species can hide in one valley. Several frogs and a toad described since 2000 are known only from around Amboli.",
    models: ["bottlenecks"],
    q: "Why is Amboli so wet, and why does that matter for wildlife?",
    a: "It sits on the edge of the Ghats where monsoon clouds first hit the hills, so the air rises, cools and drops about 7,500 mm of rain a year. The wet, misty forest suits frogs and small animals, and several species found since 2000 live only around Amboli.",
    chain: [["About 7,500 mm of rain, the most in Maharashtra", ["7,500", "wettest", "most rain", "rain"]], ["On the edge of the Ghats where clouds first hit", ["edge", "ghats", "cloud", "first hit", "rises", "cool"]], ["Wet mist suits frogs and small animals", ["frog", "mist", "wet", "small animal", "amphib"]], ["Several species found only around Amboli", ["only", "new species", "since 2000", "endemic"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why does Amboli get so much more rain than Mumbai?", o: ["It sits on the edge of the Ghats where monsoon clouds are forced up and cool", "It is closer to the sea than Mumbai", "It lies in the path of cyclones", "Its forests make their own rain clouds"] },
    bring: "When driving to Goa in the monsoon: “Amboli up there gets 7,500 mm a year, more than three times Mumbai. New frogs are still being found.”",
    follow: "Why would a frog species live in only one valley?",
    deeper: "The Hiranyakeshi river rises near Amboli from a cave temple. The Amboli ghat road links Sawantwadi on the coast to Belgaum on the plateau. Amboli's leaping frogs and bush frogs are a favourite with wildlife photographers during the rains." },

  { id: "mhp-hazur-sahib", type: "place", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Hazur Sahib: a Sikh takht in Marathwada", value: 4, cue: "Nanded, Sikh history, or why a holy city lies far from Punjab",
    emoji: "🛕", wiki: "Hazur Sahib",
    fact: "Nanded on the Godavari holds Takht Sachkhand Sri Hazur Sahib, one of the five takhts, or seats of authority, of the Sikh faith. Guru Gobind Singh, the tenth Guru, spent his last months here and died in 1708. Before his death he declared the Guru Granth Sahib the eternal Guru of the Sikhs.",
    why: "One of Sikhism's holiest places is about 1,500 km from Punjab, in the heart of Maharashtra.",
    mech: "In 1708 Guru Gobind Singh travelled south with the Mughal emperor Bahadur Shah, who was marching to the Deccan. He camped at Nanded on the Godavari. There he was attacked and wounded, and he died in October 1708. Sikhs hold that at Nanded he ended the line of human Gurus and gave that place to the scripture. In the 1830s Maharaja Ranjit Singh of Punjab paid for the present gurdwara, sending money and craftsmen. Nanded remains home to a Sikh community whose families settled here centuries ago.",
    models: ["lindy-effect"],
    q: "Why is Nanded so important in Sikh history?",
    a: "Guru Gobind Singh came south in 1708 with the Mughal emperor, camped at Nanded on the Godavari and died there after being wounded. There he declared the Guru Granth Sahib the eternal Guru. Maharaja Ranjit Singh built the present gurdwara in the 1830s, and it is one of the five takhts.",
    chain: [["Guru Gobind Singh came south in 1708", ["1708", "south", "deccan", "bahadur shah"]], ["He died at Nanded on the Godavari", ["nanded", "godavari", "died", "wounded"]], ["He declared the Guru Granth Sahib the eternal Guru", ["granth", "eternal", "scripture", "guru"]], ["Ranjit Singh built the gurdwara; one of five takhts", ["ranjit", "1830", "takht", "five"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What did Guru Gobind Singh declare at Nanded?", o: ["That the Guru Granth Sahib would be the eternal Guru", "That Nanded would be the capital of Punjab", "That a new human Guru would follow him", "That Sikhs should return to Punjab"] },
    bring: "When Nanded comes up: “It holds one of the five Sikh takhts. The tenth Guru died there in 1708 and made the holy book the final Guru.”",
    follow: "Why do you think a holy place can stay important so far from its community's homeland?",
    deeper: "Nanded celebrated 300 years of the Guru Granth Sahib's Guruship in 2008 with large gatherings. The takht keeps some weapons of Guru Gobind Singh. A special train, the Sachkhand Express, links Nanded with Amritsar." },

  { id: "mhp-jyotirlingas", type: "fact", domain: "anthropology", region: "maharashtra", title: "Five of the twelve jyotirlingas", value: 4, cue: "temple trips, Shiva temples, or why Maharashtra has so many pilgrim towns",
    emoji: "🔱", wiki: "Jyotirlinga",
    fact: "The twelve jyotirlingas are the most sacred Shiva shrines in Hindu tradition. Five are in Maharashtra: Bhimashankar near Pune, Trimbakeshwar near Nashik, Grishneshwar near Ellora, Aundha Nagnath in Hingoli and Parli Vaijnath in Beed. No other state has as many.",
    why: "Pilgrim circuits have shaped roads, markets and towns in Maharashtra for centuries.",
    mech: "The list of twelve was fixed in Sanskrit texts many centuries ago. Three of the five Maharashtra shrines stand at river sources or beside old routes. Trimbakeshwar is at the Godavari's source, Bhimashankar at the Bhima's source, and Grishneshwar beside the Ellora caves. Many were damaged in wars and rebuilt. Ahilyabai Holkar, the Holkar queen of Indore, rebuilt Grishneshwar in the 1700s and is credited with work at others. Pilgrims travelling between them created a steady trade in food, lodging and transport that continues today.",
    models: ["network-effects", "lindy-effect"],
    q: "Which jyotirlingas are in Maharashtra, and why do they matter beyond religion?",
    a: "Five of the twelve: Bhimashankar, Trimbakeshwar, Grishneshwar, Aundha Nagnath and Parli Vaijnath. Several stand at river sources or beside old routes. Ahilyabai Holkar rebuilt Grishneshwar in the 1700s. Pilgrims moving between them have supported trade in food, lodging and transport for centuries.",
    chain: [["Five of the twelve are in Maharashtra", ["five", "twelve", "12", "5"]], ["Bhimashankar, Trimbakeshwar, Grishneshwar, Aundha Nagnath, Parli Vaijnath", ["bhimashankar", "trimbak", "grishneshwar", "aundha", "parli"]], ["Ahilyabai Holkar rebuilt Grishneshwar", ["ahilyabai", "holkar", "rebuilt", "1700"]], ["Pilgrim traffic supports local trade", ["pilgrim", "trade", "lodging", "food", "transport", "market"]]],
    mcq: { q: "How many of the twelve jyotirlingas are in Maharashtra?", o: ["Five", "Two", "Eight", "One"] },
    bring: "When temple trips come up: “Maharashtra has five of the twelve jyotirlingas, more than any other state. Trimbakeshwar is at the source of the Godavari.”",
    follow: "Have you visited any of the five? Which one would you start with?",
    deeper: "The other seven are Somnath and Nageshwar in Gujarat, Mallikarjuna in Andhra Pradesh, Mahakaleshwar and Omkareshwar in Madhya Pradesh, Kedarnath in Uttarakhand, Kashi Vishwanath in Uttar Pradesh and Rameshwaram in Tamil Nadu. Some lists differ, and a few other temples also claim the title of Nageshwar or Vaidyanath." },

  { id: "mhp-sevagram", type: "place", domain: "philosophy", region: "maharashtra", title: "Sevagram: Gandhi's village of service", value: 4, cue: "Wardha, simple living, or leading from a village",
    emoji: "🧵", wiki: "Sevagram",
    fact: "In 1936 Mahatma Gandhi moved to a small village called Segaon near Wardha, at the invitation of the industrialist Jamnalal Bajaj. He lived there in a mud hut until 1946. In 1940 the village was renamed Sevagram, meaning village of service. For a decade it was the real centre of India's freedom movement.",
    why: "The most famous man in India chose to run a national movement from a village with no electricity and no proper road.",
    mech: "Gandhi believed India lived in its villages, and that leaders should live as villagers did. Wardha is near the centre of India, so people could reach it from every side. Bajaj gave land and money. The huts were built from mud, bamboo and local wood, so anyone could copy them. Congress leaders travelled to Sevagram for key meetings, including those that led to the Quit India call of 1942. Gandhi also used the ashram to test ideas on hygiene, spinning, village industry and basic education.",
    models: ["first-principles", "inversion"],
    q: "Why did Gandhi make Sevagram his base, and what did he do there?",
    a: "He believed leaders should live like villagers, so in 1936 he moved to Segaon near Wardha, where Jamnalal Bajaj gave land. Wardha is central, so leaders could reach it, and the mud huts were cheap to copy. Key Congress meetings, including those before Quit India in 1942, were held there, and he tested village industry and education.",
    chain: [["Moved to Segaon near Wardha in 1936 with Bajaj's help", ["1936", "wardha", "bajaj", "segaon"]], ["Leaders should live as villagers do", ["village", "live", "simpl", "mud", "hut"]], ["Central location, reachable from all sides", ["centre", "center", "central", "reach"]], ["Key meetings and experiments in village work", ["meeting", "quit india", "spinning", "education", "industry", "test"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Who invited Gandhi to settle near Wardha?", o: ["Jamnalal Bajaj", "Jawaharlal Nehru", "The British Viceroy", "Rabindranath Tagore"] },
    bring: "When someone talks about leading from the top: “Gandhi ran the freedom movement from a mud hut in Sevagram for ten years.”",
    follow: "What would change if your organisation's leader worked from the shop floor?",
    deeper: "Gandhi's hut, Bapu Kuti, still stands with his few belongings. The village's name was changed partly because letters kept going to Shegaon in Buldhana, another holy town. The Bajaj family's companies still have deep roots in Wardha." },

  { id: "mhp-nagpur-zero-mile", type: "fact", domain: "geography", region: "maharashtra", title: "Nagpur's zero mile and its oranges", value: 3, cue: "Nagpur, the centre of India, or oranges",
    emoji: "🍊", wiki: "Zero Mile Stone (Nagpur)",
    fact: "A sandstone pillar in Nagpur, the Zero Mile Stone, was set up by the British in 1907 during the Great Trigonometrical Survey. Distances to other cities were measured from it, and Nagpur is often called the centre of India. The city is also known as the Orange City, and the Nagpur orange got a GI tag in 2014.",
    why: "Being in the middle of the map shaped Nagpur's history, from a British survey to a state's second capital.",
    mech: "The Great Trigonometrical Survey mapped India with chains of triangles measured from fixed points. Nagpur, near the middle of the country, was a natural base, and the pillar marks a survey point. Because the city is central, railways from all four directions met here. In 1960 Nagpur became Maharashtra's second capital, and the state assembly meets there each winter. The orange grows well in Vidarbha's black soil and hot, dry climate, and the city became its main market.",
    models: ["network-effects"],
    q: "Why is Nagpur called the centre of India, and why is it the Orange City?",
    a: "A 1907 survey pillar, the Zero Mile Stone, marks a point of the Great Trigonometrical Survey, and the city lies near the middle of India. That central spot made it a railway junction and Maharashtra's second capital. Vidarbha's soil and dry climate suit oranges, which got a GI tag in 2014.",
    chain: [["The Zero Mile Stone from the 1907 survey", ["1907", "zero mile", "survey", "pillar"]], ["Near the middle of India", ["centre", "center", "middle", "central"]], ["A railway junction and the state's second capital", ["railway", "junction", "second capital", "winter"]], ["Oranges from Vidarbha's soil and climate, GI tag 2014", ["orange", "2014", "soil", "climate"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What does Nagpur's Zero Mile Stone mark?", o: ["A survey point from the 1907 Great Trigonometrical Survey", "The exact centre of Maharashtra", "The start of India's first highway", "The place where oranges were first planted"] },
    bring: "When someone mentions Nagpur: “It has a stone from 1907 that the British used as a zero point. Two villages in Madhya Pradesh also claim to be India's centre.”",
    follow: "What advantages come from being in the middle of a country?",
    deeper: "The Nagpur orange is a mandarin, loose-skinned and easy to peel. Two villages in Madhya Pradesh, Karaundi and Barsali, also claim to be India's geographic centre. Nagpur is also home to Deekshabhoomi and the headquarters of the RSS." },

  { id: "mhp-deekshabhoomi", type: "event", domain: "philosophy", region: "maharashtra", title: "Deekshabhoomi: 14 October 1956", value: 5, cue: "Dr Ambedkar, Nagpur, or changing your path with dignity",
    emoji: "☸️", wiki: "Deekshabhoomi",
    fact: "On 14 October 1956, at Nagpur, Dr B. R. Ambedkar embraced Buddhism along with several lakh followers, mostly from Dalit communities. Estimates range from about 3.8 lakh to over 5 lakh people. It was one of the largest religious conversions in history. The spot is now Deekshabhoomi, marked by a large white stupa.",
    why: "Dr Ambedkar had said in 1935 that he would not die a Hindu. He kept that word twenty-one years later, and he did it with a crowd, not alone.",
    mech: "Dr Ambedkar had fought caste discrimination for decades, from the Mahad Satyagraha of 1927 to writing the Constitution. He studied religions for years and chose Buddhism because it was born in India and taught equality and reason. He chose Nagpur partly because it was central and had a strong following. He took the vows from a monk, then gave them to the crowd, along with 22 vows of his own. He died less than two months later, on 6 December 1956. The stupa, finished in 2001, is one of the largest hollow stupas in Asia.",
    models: ["commitment-consistency", "first-principles"],
    q: "What happened at Deekshabhoomi, and why did Dr Ambedkar choose Buddhism?",
    a: "On 14 October 1956 at Nagpur, Dr Ambedkar and several lakh followers embraced Buddhism. After decades fighting caste discrimination, he chose a religion born in India that taught equality and reason. He gave the crowd 22 vows and died less than two months later. A large stupa, finished in 2001, marks the spot.",
    chain: [["14 October 1956 at Nagpur", ["14 october", "1956", "nagpur"]], ["Several lakh followers converted together", ["lakh", "followers", "crowd", "largest"]], ["Buddhism chosen for equality and reason, after decades of struggle", ["equality", "reason", "caste", "born in india", "chose"]], ["22 vows; the stupa finished in 2001", ["22 vows", "vows", "stupa", "2001"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What happened at Nagpur on 14 October 1956?", o: ["Dr Ambedkar and several lakh followers embraced Buddhism", "The Constitution of India was signed", "Maharashtra state was formed", "Dr Ambedkar was elected to Parliament"] },
    bring: "When Dr Ambedkar comes up: “He promised in 1935 not to die a Hindu. In 1956 at Nagpur he kept it, with lakhs of people beside him.”",
    follow: "What promise have you kept for more than twenty years?",
    deeper: "The day is marked each year as Dhammachakra Pravartan Din, and lakhs visit Nagpur for it. The date was chosen for Vijayadashami, which is also linked by tradition to Emperor Ashoka's turn to Buddhism. Maharashtra has by far the largest Buddhist population of any Indian state." },

  { id: "mhp-jayakwadi", type: "place", domain: "economics", region: "maharashtra", title: "Paithan and the Jayakwadi dam", value: 3, cue: "Marathwada drought, Paithani saris, or who gets the river's water",
    emoji: "🌾", wiki: "Jayakwadi Dam",
    fact: "Paithan on the Godavari was once Pratishthana, capital of the Satavahana kings over 2,000 years ago. It is still known for the Paithani silk sari. Just upstream stands the Jayakwadi dam, opened in 1976. It is about 10 km long, one of the longest earthen dams in Asia, and its lake, Nath Sagar, supplies water to Marathwada.",
    why: "The dam decides whether Marathwada's farms and cities have water. Every dry year it starts a fight between upstream and downstream districts.",
    mech: "Marathwada lies in the rain shadow of the Western Ghats and gets far less rain than the coast. The Godavari brings water from the wetter hills near Nashik. Jayakwadi stores it for irrigation, for Aurangabad's homes and for industry. But dams upstream in Nashik and Ahilyanagar fill first. In dry years little flows down, and Jayakwadi stays low. Rules now require upstream dams to release water when Jayakwadi falls below a set level, and these releases are often disputed.",
    models: ["bottlenecks", "game-theory"],
    q: "Why is the Jayakwadi dam so important, and why is it often in the news?",
    a: "Marathwada is dry, in the rain shadow of the Ghats, so it depends on Godavari water stored at Jayakwadi, a 10 km earthen dam opened in 1976 near Paithan. Dams upstream in Nashik and Ahilyanagar fill first, so in dry years Jayakwadi stays low and upstream districts must release water, which causes disputes.",
    chain: [["Marathwada is dry, in the rain shadow", ["rain shadow", "dry", "less rain", "marathwada"]], ["Jayakwadi, a 10 km earthen dam opened in 1976, stores Godavari water", ["1976", "10 km", "earthen", "nath sagar", "stores"]], ["Upstream dams fill first", ["upstream", "nashik", "fill first", "ahilyanagar"]], ["Releases to Jayakwadi are disputed", ["release", "dispute", "fight", "below"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why does Jayakwadi often run low in dry years?", o: ["Dams upstream in Nashik and Ahilyanagar fill first and release little", "The dam leaks badly", "Farmers refuse to use its water", "The Godavari changes course every year"] },
    bring: "When Marathwada's drought is in the news: “It comes down to Jayakwadi. If Nashik's dams do not release water, Paithan's lake stays empty.”",
    follow: "Who should get a river's water first: the people upstream or downstream?",
    deeper: "The Paithani sari uses pure silk with gold or silver zari, and its border and pallu are woven by hand with a tapestry method, which can take months. Paithan was a great trading city in Satavahana times and is also the home of Sant Eknath. Nath Sagar is a bird sanctuary that draws flamingos in winter." },

  { id: "mhp-kalsubai", type: "place", domain: "geography", region: "maharashtra", title: "Kalsubai and Bhandardara: the roof of Maharashtra", value: 3, cue: "trekking, highest peaks, or old dams",
    emoji: "⛰️", wiki: "Kalsubai",
    fact: "Kalsubai, at 1,646 metres, is the highest peak in Maharashtra. It stands above Bhandardara in Ahilyanagar district, about 180 km from Mumbai. Below it, the Wilson Dam on the Pravara river was finished in 1910 and is one of the oldest large dams in India.",
    why: "The state's highest point is a half-day trek that thousands of Mumbai office-goers finish on weekends.",
    mech: "Kalsubai is part of the main ridge of the Sahyadris, which here rise steeply from the Konkan. The peak is a hard cap of Deccan lava, left standing while softer layers wore away. The trek from Bari village is about 6 km, with iron ladders fixed on the steep rock sections. A small temple to the goddess Kalsubai sits on top. The Wilson Dam below is a masonry dam about 80 metres high, built to irrigate the dry Pravara valley. Its lake, Arthur Lake, fills with the monsoon rain that soaks the ridge.",
    models: ["lindy-effect"],
    q: "What makes Kalsubai and Bhandardara worth knowing?",
    a: "Kalsubai is Maharashtra's highest peak at 1,646 metres, a hard cap of Deccan lava on the Sahyadri ridge. The trek from Bari uses iron ladders on the steep parts. Below it, the Wilson Dam of 1910 holds Arthur Lake and irrigates the Pravara valley.",
    chain: [["Highest peak in Maharashtra at 1,646 metres", ["1,646", "highest", "peak"]], ["A hard cap of Deccan lava on the Sahyadri ridge", ["lava", "deccan", "ridge", "hard", "basalt"]], ["A 6 km trek with iron ladders", ["trek", "ladder", "bari", "6 km"]], ["Wilson Dam of 1910 and Arthur Lake", ["wilson", "1910", "arthur", "dam", "pravara"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What is the highest peak in Maharashtra?", o: ["Kalsubai, 1,646 metres", "Mahabaleshwar's Wilson Point", "Harishchandragad", "Salher"] },
    bring: "When trekking comes up: “Kalsubai is the top of Maharashtra, 1,646 metres. Ladders on the rock, and a temple at the top.”",
    follow: "Have you climbed the highest point of any state?",
    deeper: "The nearby Randha Falls on the Pravara drop about 45 metres. Bhandardara is popular in June for fireflies and in the monsoon for the Umbrella Falls at the dam. Salher in Nashik district, at about 1,567 metres, is the highest fort in the state." },

  { id: "mhp-konkan-kada", type: "place", domain: "science", region: "maharashtra", title: "Konkan Kada: a cliff that bends inward", value: 3, cue: "Harishchandragad, dramatic viewpoints, or rainbows in the mist",
    emoji: "🌈", wiki: "Harishchandragad",
    fact: "Konkan Kada is a huge cliff on Harishchandragad fort, on the border of Ahilyanagar and Thane districts. It is curved inward like a cobra's hood and drops hundreds of metres towards the Konkan. In the monsoon, trekkers sometimes see a circular rainbow in the clouds below, called a Brocken spectre or glory.",
    why: "A natural wonder a few hours from Mumbai shows a rare optical effect that most people only read about.",
    mech: "The Sahyadri edge is made of flat layers of Deccan lava. Softer layers below wear away faster than hard layers above, so the cliff face is undercut and curves inward. Wind rushing up the concave face is strong enough to push light objects back up. When the sun is low behind a person and mist fills the valley in front, the tiny water droplets bend light back towards the viewer. This forms a ring of colours around the person's shadow. The same effect is seen from aeroplanes, around the plane's shadow on a cloud.",
    models: ["first-principles"],
    q: "Why does Konkan Kada curve inward, and what is the rainbow seen there?",
    a: "The cliff is made of lava layers; softer lower layers wear away faster, so the face is undercut and concave, and wind rushes up it. When the sun is behind a person and mist fills the valley, droplets bend light back to form a ring of colours around the person's shadow, called a glory or Brocken spectre.",
    chain: [["A concave cliff on Harishchandragad", ["concave", "curve", "inward", "cobra", "harishchandragad"]], ["Softer layers wear away faster, undercutting the face", ["soft", "layer", "wear", "undercut", "lava"]], ["Wind rushes up the curved face", ["wind", "rush", "push"]], ["Sun behind, mist in front: a ring of colours around your shadow", ["mist", "droplet", "shadow", "ring", "glory", "brocken", "sun"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What is needed to see the circular rainbow at Konkan Kada?", o: ["The sun behind you and mist filling the valley in front", "A full moon and a clear sky", "Heavy rain falling on the cliff top", "Snow on the peaks"] },
    bring: "When someone shows a Harishchandragad photo: “If the sun is behind you and the valley is full of mist, you can see a ring of colours around your own shadow.”",
    follow: "Have you ever seen a rainbow that was a full circle?",
    deeper: "Harishchandragad also has the Kedareshwar cave, where a large Shiva linga stands in cold water, surrounded by four pillars, one of which is broken. The fort's temple is about a thousand years old. Trekkers warn that the cliff edge is unfenced and deaths have occurred." },

  { id: "mhp-thibaw", type: "historical story", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "The king of Burma in Ratnagiri", value: 4, cue: "Ratnagiri, exile, or how empires swap prisoners", story: "mhp-thibaw",
    emoji: "👑", wiki: "Thibaw Palace",
    fact: "In 1885 the British defeated King Thibaw, the last king of Burma, and sent him into exile in Ratnagiri in 1886. He lived there for thirty years and died in 1916. The British built him a palace of red laterite stone in 1910. It still stands as the Thibaw Palace museum.",
    why: "The British also exiled the last Mughal emperor, Bahadur Shah Zafar, to Rangoon in Burma. Each empire's last king died in the other's land.",
    mech: "The British took Upper Burma in a short war in November 1885. A king left at home could become a centre of revolt, so he was sent far away, to a place few Burmese could reach. Ratnagiri was a small Konkan port with a British officer and no Burmese community. Thibaw lived first in rented houses, with his queen Supayalat and daughters, on a fixed allowance. The palace came only in 1910, and he lived in it for six years. One daughter married an Indian from the household. The family's descendants still live in the Konkan.",
    models: ["inversion", "game-theory"],
    q: "Why did the last king of Burma live and die in Ratnagiri?",
    a: "After the British took Burma in 1885, they exiled King Thibaw to Ratnagiri in 1886 so he could not become a centre of revolt. He lived there on an allowance with Queen Supayalat and their daughters, moved into a laterite palace built in 1910 and died in 1916. The British had earlier sent the last Mughal emperor to Burma.",
    chain: [["The British took Burma in 1885 and exiled the king", ["1885", "burma", "british", "exile", "defeated"]], ["A far place where he could not lead a revolt", ["revolt", "far", "centre", "center", "reach"]], ["Thirty years in Ratnagiri; palace built 1910; died 1916", ["ratnagiri", "1910", "1916", "thirty", "palace", "allowance"]], ["The last Mughal was exiled to Burma", ["mughal", "zafar", "rangoon"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why was King Thibaw sent to Ratnagiri?", o: ["To keep him far from Burma so he could not lead a revolt", "He asked to live near the sea", "The British wanted him to rule the Konkan", "He was a prisoner of the Marathas"] },
    bring: "When Ratnagiri comes up: “The last king of Burma died there in 1916. And the last Mughal emperor died in Burma. The British swapped them.”",
    follow: "What would it feel like to live thirty years in a place where no one spoke your language?",
    deeper: "Amitav Ghosh's novel The Glass Palace tells this story. Thibaw's tomb is in Ratnagiri, and Myanmar's president visited it in 2012. The palace now also houses a museum about the Konkan and its famous people." },

  { id: "mhp-lavasa", type: "business concept", domain: "business", region: "maharashtra", title: "Lavasa: a city that stalled", value: 4, cue: "real estate dreams, big projects, or buying before the papers are clear",
    emoji: "🏗️", wiki: "Lavasa",
    fact: "Lavasa was planned as India's first private hill city, built by Hindustan Construction Company in the hills near Pune. It was modelled on the Italian town of Portofino, with colourful buildings around a lake. In November 2010 the central environment ministry ordered work to stop. In 2018 the company went into insolvency, leaving buyers with unfinished homes.",
    why: "Thousands of people paid for flats in a city that half exists. It is a lesson about permissions, debt and promises.",
    mech: "The project began in the 2000s with state approvals but without clearance from the central environment ministry, which the company argued it did not need. Building on steep hill slopes raised concerns about forests and the lake. A public interest case led to the stop-work order in 2010. Work resumed with conditions in 2011, but the long pause scared buyers and lenders. The company had borrowed heavily and its sales fell. Debts mounted until lenders took it to the insolvency court in 2018. Years later, flat owners still wait for a resolution.",
    models: ["margin-of-safety", "second-order-thinking"],
    q: "Why did Lavasa fail?",
    a: "It was built with state permission but without clearance from the central environment ministry, on steep hill slopes near a lake. A court case led to a stop-work order in 2010. The pause hurt sales, debt mounted, and lenders took the company into insolvency in 2018, leaving buyers waiting.",
    chain: [["A private hill city near Pune, modelled on Portofino", ["portofino", "hill city", "private", "pune", "hcc"]], ["Built without central environment clearance", ["clearance", "environment", "permission", "approval"]], ["Stop-work order in 2010 hurt sales", ["2010", "stop", "order", "sales", "pause"]], ["Debt mounted, insolvency in 2018", ["debt", "2018", "insolvency", "lender", "borrow"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What stopped work at Lavasa in 2010?", o: ["An order from the central environment ministry over missing clearance", "An earthquake damaged the dam", "The company ran out of cement", "Residents voted to stop construction"] },
    bring: "When someone is excited about a new township: “Remember Lavasa. Beautiful plans, state approvals, and one missing clearance stopped the whole city.”",
    follow: "What checks would you make before paying for a home in a brand-new project?",
    deeper: "Lavasa sits by the Warasgaon lake, part of Pune's water supply. The project planned for about 2 lakh residents across several towns. A resolution plan for the company was approved in 2023, but disputes over it continued." }

